<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mathildenhöhe</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mathildenhöhe is a hill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, where Grand Duke Ernest Louis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt-artists-colony">Darmstadt Artists' Colony</a> in 1899. Its artists' houses, exhibition building, Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> chapel and Wedding Tower, built largely by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-maria-olbrich">Joseph Maria Olbrich</a>, form an outstanding ensemble of Jugendstil architecture, listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> as a World Heritage Site in 2021.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mathildenhohe.jpg" alt="Mathildenhöhe" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Wolf, www.foto-tw.de · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darmstadt_Mathildenh%C3%B6he.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
